<text x="0" y="-2.2" size="1.27" layer="25" ratio="10" align="top-center">&gt;NAME</text>
<text x="0" y="5.2" size="1.27" layer="27" ratio="10" align="bottom-center">&gt;VALUE</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-3" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">Molex 105017-0001 micro-USB-B, bottom mount, B type, flangeless.</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-3.5" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">Rebuilt from SD-105017-001 sheet 1, RECOMMENDED P.C.B. PATTERN</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-4" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">LAYOUT, measured off the drawing's vector geometry. Scale set by</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-4.5" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">the 5.00 between hole centres, checked against the 1.45 land</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-5" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">(1.4467 measured) and the 0.85 drill (0.8426). Origin is the rear</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-5.5" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">row centreline; +y is the PCB EDGE and the mouth.</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-6.3" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">LAYER 48 carries the PCB EDGE line at y +4.141. It must fall on</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-6.8" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">the board outline -- check_board.py enforces it. The mating face</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-7.3" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">is 0.70 further out at +4.841, so the shell OVERHANGS the edge.</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-8.1" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">MS1/MS2 are PLATED SLOTS, 0.60 x 1.30 at x +/-3.50, drawn on LAYER</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-8.6" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">46 as ZERO-WIDTH outlines, which is what JLCPCB's own Eagle article</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-9.1" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">asks for. Their CAM job merges layer 46 into the GKO outline, so</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-9.6" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">the slot reaches them -- but a slot in the outline layer does not</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-10.1" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">say whether it is plated. ADD AN ORDER NOTE naming these two as</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-10.6" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">PLATED. The 0.60 pad drill inside each slot is intentional: drill</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-11.1" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">first, then rout. STILL UNCONFIRMED: a plated slot sitting INSIDE</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-11.6" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">an SMD land. Nothing JLCPCB publishes covers it -- ask them.</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-12.4" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">PIN 1 IS AT +X. Corrected 2026-08-28. The drawing's RECOMMENDED P.C.B.</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-12.9" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">PATTERN is a TOP view with the connector front at -y and PIN 1 at -x. This</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-13.4" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">footprint puts the front at +y, which is a 180 degree rotation of that view,</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-13.9" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">so PIN 1 has to move to +x as well. It was built by flipping y only, which</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-14.4" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">MIRRORS the pin order: VBUS landed on the shell's GND contact and GND on</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-14.9" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">VBUS -- a dead short across the cable on plug-in -- while D- drove the ID</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-15.4" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">contact and the real D- contact sat on X1.4, which is not connected. Only D+</text>
<text x="-4.9" y="-15.9" size="0.4" layer="51" ratio="10">was right, because it is the centre pad and survives the mirror.</text>
</package>
